# Conversation-Level Cyberbullying Detection  Code Archive

Manraj Boparai May 2026  
COM3001 Professional Project

## Environment

- Python 3.12.3
- CUDA 13.0
- NVIDIA RTX 4000 Ada, 20 GB VRAM
- University of Surrey Otter lab machines: `otter133`–`otter162`
- Linux

## Installation
Create and activate a virtual environment:
```bash
python -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
```
For the Gemma 3-27B GGUF backend, additionally install `llama-cpp-python` :

```bash
pip install llama-cpp-python
```

## Cache directory and model paths

Several scripts reference the following Otter lab machine paths:

```text
/scratch/mb02997/hf
/scratch/mb02997/models/gemma-3-27b-it.IQ4_XS.gguf
```
These were the working paths used during the project.

To run the code on a different machine, update the cache/model paths in:

```text
src/finetuning/lora/llama_lora.py
src/finetuning/lorma/llama_lorma_plus_akaiming_b0.py
src/finetuning/lorma/llama_lorma_plus_a0_bkaiming.py
src/framework/config.py
```

The Gemma 3-27B IQ4_XS GGUF file is approximately 14 GB and is not included in this archive.

Download location:

```text
https://huggingface.co/mradermacher/gemma-3-27b-it-GGUF
```

Required file:

```text
gemma-3-27b-it.IQ4_XS.gguf
```

## Dataset

The pre-split CSV files are stored in:

```text
data_processed/
```

This directory contains:

```text
train.csv
val.csv
test.csv
```

The dataset is derived from splits:

```text
surrey-nlp/Cyberbullying-Detection-CB2
```

## Running prompting experiments

Example commands:

```bash
python run_experiment.py --model llama --strategy zero_shot
python run_experiment.py --model gemma --strategy zero_shot_meta
python run_experiment.py --model all --strategy zero_shot
python run_experiment.py --model llama --strategy all
```

Available models:

```text
llama
gemma
qwen
mistral
```
Available prompting strategies:

```text
zero_shot
few_shot
zero_shot_peer
few_shot_peer
zero_shot_intent
zero_shot_aggressive
zero_shot_meta
few_shot_meta
```

Results are saved to:

```text
outputs/results/
```

This directory is created automatically on the first run.

## Running the classical baseline

Run the TF-IDF + logistic regression baseline with:

```bash
python -m baselines.tfidf_lr
```

## Running fine-tuning

Run the LoRA baseline:

```bash
python -m src.finetuning.lora.llama_lora
```

Run the LoRMA+ paper-initialisation variant:

```bash
python -m src.finetuning.lorma.llama_lorma_plus_akaiming_b0
```

Run the LoRMA+ reference-code initialisation variant:

```bash
python -m src.finetuning.lorma.llama_lorma_plus_a0_bkaiming
```

Each fine-tuning run takes approximately 3–6 hours on a single 20 GB GPU.

Trained adapters are saved to:

```text
outputs/<run_name>/
```

## File map

```text
src/framework/
    config.py
        Model registry, paths, and strategy names.

    data.py
        CSV loading, conversation parsing, and metadata extraction.

    prompts.py
        Base prompt, message construction, and strategy dispatch.

    few_shot_examples.py
        The four few-shot demonstrations.

    models.py
        Hugging Face Transformers and llama.cpp inference backends.

    evaluate.py
        Macro F1, per-class precision/recall/F1, and confusion matrix calculation.

src/finetuning/
    lora/
        llama_lora.py
            LoRA fine-tuning baseline.

    lorma/
        llama_lorma_plus_akaiming_b0.py
            LoRMA+ paper-initialisation variant.

        llama_lorma_plus_a0_bkaiming.py
            LoRMA+ reference-implementation initialisation variant.

baselines/
    tfidf_lr.py
        TF-IDF + logistic regression baseline.

legacy/
    llama_zero_shot.py
    llama_few_shot.py
    llama_few_shot_meta.py
        Three legacy per-script implementations retained as evidence of the
        framework refactor discussed in Section 3.2 of the report.

run_experiment.py
    Unified prompting runner.

data_processed/
    Pre-split CSV files.

results_summary.txt
    Aggregated results table mirroring Appendix A.4.
```

## Full results

`FYP.txt` contains the full per-configuration results used for the report tables.

It includes:

```text
macro F1
accuracy
class-wise precision
class-wise recall
class-wise F1
confusion matrix values
```

These results support Tables 4.1, 4.2, 4.3, and A.4 of the report.

## Notes

The prompting experiments are run through the unified `run_experiment.py` framework.

The fine-tuning experiments and classical baseline are run through separate scripts, but they use the same evaluation approach for reporting metrics and confusion matrices.